<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Alaiye Beylik</h1><p class="st-meta">1293 CE – 1471 CE (approx.) · capital Alanya</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A small Turkish principality at Alanya on the Mediterranean coast, ruled by a branch of the Karamanids under the great Seljuk citadel.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alanya">Alanya</a> was built up by the Seljuk sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kayqubad-i">Kayqubad I</a> after 1221 as a winter capital and naval base. After Seljuk authority collapsed in the late 13th century, local Turkish lords, later linked to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/karamanids">Karamanids</a>, took over the city and its surrounding coast.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The rulers lived in the Seljuk citadel and used its shipyard and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-tower-alanya">Red Tower</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a> visited in the 1330s and met its ruler Yusuf Bey. The town traded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> and other goods with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>. In 1427 its ruler sold <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alanya">Alanya</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barsbay">Barsbay</a>, though Mamluk control did not last, and the Karamanid line returned.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The Ottoman commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gedik-ahmed-pasha">Gedik Ahmed Pasha</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alanya">Alanya</a> in 1471 from its last ruler, Kilij Arslan, who received a domain elsewhere.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="emirate, 1293 CE – 1471 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V62 Q24 40 50 34 Q76 40 76 62 V82 Z"/><path d="M50 34 V24"/><path d="M53 13 A6 6 0 1 0 53 25 A4.5 4.5 0 1 1 53 13"/><path d="M38 82 V66 Q44 58 50 66 V82"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">emirate</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Anatolia and the Levant</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:85.43%;width:3.54%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1293 CE – 1471 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Alanya</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Yusuf Bey, Lutfi Bey, Kilij Arslan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Turkish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Beylik of Alanya, Karamanids of Alanya</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1382&amp;focus=alaiye-beylik" aria-label="Alaiye Beylik on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5484 860 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Alaiye Beylik lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5804 1116L5832 1095M5804 1116L5815 1335M5804 1116L5709 970"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5832 1095h0M5815 1335h0M5709 970h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5804 1116h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5804 1116h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Alanya. <a href="/atlas?year=1382&amp;focus=alaiye-beylik">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

